.\" Title: vrw-cat
.\" Author: vrw contributors
.\" Manual section: 1
.\" Process this file with
.\"   groff -man -Tascii vrw-cat.1

.TH VRW-CAT 1 "2025" "vrw" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
vrw cat \- print the VTTY buffer of a running command

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vrw cat
[\fITARGET\fR] [\fB\-\-plain\fR] [\fB\-\-interactive\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Prints the virtual terminal (VTTY) buffer of a running command to stdout. By default, the output includes ANSI escape sequences so colors, bold, underline, strikethrough, and other formatting is preserved exactly as the child process displayed it.

When no target is specified and exactly one command is running, that command is selected automatically. If multiple commands are running, a target must be specified either by PID (numeric) or by command name.

The output includes both the visible screen content and the scrollback history.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.BR \-\-plain
Output plain text without any ANSI escape sequences or formatting. Strips all colors, bold, underline, and other attributes, producing clean text suitable for piping into \fBgrep\fR(1), \fBawk\fR(1), \fBless\fR(1), or redirecting to a file.

.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help information and exit.

.TP
.BR \-\-interactive
Present a numbered list of commands and let the user select
which ones to cat by typing space-separated numbers (e.g. "1 3 5").
Requires a terminal (stdin must be a TTY).

.SH TARGET SELECTION

If \fITARGET\fR is provided:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
Numeric values are treated as a process ID (PID).
.IP \(bu 4
Non-numeric values are matched case-insensitively against command names.
.RE

If no \fITARGET\fR is provided and exactly one command is running, it is selected automatically. If multiple commands are running, an error is printed listing available commands.

.SH EXAMPLES

Print colored output of the only running command:
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrw cat\fR
.RE

Print plain text output (no colors):
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrw cat --plain\fR
.RE

Target a specific command by PID:
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrw cat 12345\fR
.RE

Target by name (colored by default):
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrw cat htop\fR
.RE

Save plain output to a file:
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrw cat --plain my-server > output.log\fR
.RE

Search for a pattern in plain command output:
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrw cat --plain db-console | grep "ERROR"\fR
.RE

View colored output in a pager:
.RS
.PP
\fB$ vrw cat vim-session | less -R\fR
.RE

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vrw (1),
.BR vrw-screenshot (1),
